  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nBreezy southwesterly winds today have advected in warmer and\nmoister air, pushing high temperatures to the mid to upper 90s\nfor much of the Quad State with dew points in the lower to\nmiddle 70s. Heat indices are likely to reach 102 to 108 today.\nHowever, convective activity is increasing and areas which \nreceive moderate to heavy rain and/or thunderstorms will see an \nearlier end to heat issues today. An areawide Heat Advisory \nremains in effect until 8pm today.\n\nScattered shower and thunderstorm activity is forecast through\nthe afternoon into the early evening as a boundary progresses\nsouthward across the Quad State. Later in the evening into the\novernight hours, this boundary shifts back northward with\nsmaller chances of showers and storms. Shower/storm potential\npeaks late night into the morning hours Thursday in the north,\nwith the cold front then shifting back southward through the\nQuad State during the day Thursday. \n\nDecent instability is forecast today, though shear is limited. \nA few strong to severe storms will be possible this afternoon \nthrough the early evening, mainly diurnally driven with \n1000-1200J/kg DCAPE making downburst winds the primary severe \nweather hazard. A marginal risk covers nearly all of the Quad \nState today. Another marginal risk area covers SEMO and adjacent\nportions of WKY and SIL for tomorrow's activity, but model \nsupport is less consistent for that timeframe. Should stronger\nstorms materialize tomorrow, damaging wind gusts will again be\nthe primary severe weather hazard. \n\nThere is some potential for training showers and storms, with\nthe best potential across the north late tonight into tomorrow.\nQPF totals of a half inch to an inch are forecast, with\nlocalized higher amounts possible. Given recent dry weather, it\nis unlikely that any flooding issues develop beyond nuisance\nponding on roads. A marginal risk ERO covers the Quad State\ntoday and tomorrow. \n\nWith the front positioned near the KY/TN border Friday, rain\nchances are increasing and highest in Western Kentucky (50-70%).\nTemperatures finally fall to near normal Friday with highs in\nthe mid-80s. Don't get too used to it though as the front lifts\nback north Saturday and the weekend to early next week sees a\nreturn to highs in the lower 90s. Saturday precip chances are\nsplit between the Southern Pennyrile seeing continued moisture\nadvection from the Lower Mississippi, and northwestern areas\npossibly seeing showers from a weakening system approaching \nfrom the northwest.\n\nSunday and Monday are currently projected to be dry, in the\nmiddle of systems in the southeastern U.S. and a front \nstretching from the Front Range to the Upper Midwest. Upper \nMidwest low pressure moving eastward should cause that front to\ndrift towards the Quad State, with a chance of showers and \nthunderstorms Tuesday and Wednesday.",
